//
// `reduce` reads its accumulator type off the FOLD, not off the initial value (loft#956).
//
// The refusal of a collection accumulator used to ask the INITIAL VALUE what type it was, and a
// bare `[]` has none to give — it arrives as `Unknown(0)`, which is not heap storage, so it
// walked straight past the refusal it was written for.  What came out the other side was a
// `reduce_acc` variable with no type: `--interpret` read it back EMPTY and `--native` reached
// `rust_type` with a `Never` and panicked — an internal compiler error with the user's line
// nowhere in it.  Both on released 2026.8.0 as well.
//
// The signature always knew.  `f: fn(U, T) -> U` names `U` in its first parameter and the init
// only has to be assignable to it, so the accumulator comes from the fold and the literal only
// has to agree.  c1 and c2 are then the SAME program said two ways, and they get the same
// answer — which is the whole point: `[]` and `vector<integer>` were never different folds,
// only different amounts of writing-out.
//
// @PLN165 E7 changed what that same answer IS.  `reduce` is a `#builtin` method whose
// declaration folds with its own body (`acc = init; for x in self { acc = f(acc, x); } acc`),
// so a collection accumulator is no longer refused: every cell below folds, and c1 and c2 still
// answer identically.  c4's bare `[]` at a KEYED accumulator reached the declaration as a bare
// `null` (the generic call built an empty vector for a `[]` argument and nothing for a keyed
// one): the interpreter looped and `--native` did not compile.  It now builds the empty
// collection its concrete spelling builds.

struct Rec956 { id: integer }

fn addv956(acc: vector<integer>, x: integer) -> vector<integer> { acc += [x * 10]; acc }
fn addt956(acc: vector<text>, x: text) -> vector<text> { acc += ["{x}{x}"]; acc }
fn addi956(acc: index<Rec956[id]>, x: integer) -> index<Rec956[id]> { acc += [Rec956 { id: x }]; acc }

fn main() {
  n = [1, 2, 3, 4];
  ts = ["a", "b"];

  // c1 — the filed shape: a bare `[]`.  Nothing here says what it is empty OF; the fold does.
  c1 = n.reduce([], addv956);

  // c2 (oracle) — the same fold with the accumulator written out.  c1 must answer identically.
  init: vector<integer> = [];
  c2 = n.reduce(init, addv956);

  // c3 — the element type is not the axis: a `vector<text>` fold from a bare `[]`.
  c3 = ts.reduce([], addt956);

  // c4 — a KEYED collection, not just a vector: a bare `[]` is how every kind is spelled empty.
  c4 = n.reduce([], addi956);

  assert("{c1}" == "{c2}" && "{c1}" == "[10,20,30,40]", "{c1} {c2}");
  assert("{c3}" == "[\"aa\",\"bb\"]", "{c3}");
  assert(len(c4) == 4 && c4[3].id == 3, "{len(c4)}");
}
